== Chapter IV

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[Indwelling sin is enmity against God. Its power shown from this. It admits of no peace or rest. It is against God himself. It shows itself in aversion from God and in a bent toward evil. It is universal: to all of God, in all of the soul. It is constant.]
]

#smallcaps[WE] have seen the #emph[seat] and #emph[subject] of this law of sin. Next we might take a view of its #emph[nature] in general, which will also show its power and efficacy. But I will not enlarge on this, since it is not my business to declare the #emph[nature] of indwelling sin, and others have also done it. So, with respect only to the particular aim we have in hand, I will consider #emph[one property] of it that belongs to its nature, and that always, wherever it is. This is what the apostle expresses in Rom 8:7: “the mind of the flesh is enmity against God.” What is called here φρόνημα τῆς σαρκός, “the wisdom of the flesh,” is the same as the #emph[law of sin] that we are dwelling on. And what does he say of it? Why, it is ἔχθρα εἰς Θεόν, “enmity against God.” It is not only an #emph[enemy], for then some #emph[reconciliation] of it to God might possibly be made; it is #emph[enmity] itself, and so is not capable of accepting any terms of peace. #emph[Enemies] may be reconciled, but enmity cannot. Indeed, the only way to reconcile #emph[enemies] is to destroy the #emph[enmity]. So the apostle tells us in another case (Rom 5:10): “when we were enemies of God, we were reconciled to Him.” That is a work achieved and brought about by the blood of Christ: the reconciling of the greatest enemies. But when he comes to speak of #emph[enmity], there is no way with it except that it must be #emph[abolished] and destroyed (Eph 2:15): “by abolishing in His flesh the enmity.” There is no way to deal with any #emph[enmity] whatever except by abolishing or destroying it.

And this also lies in it as it is #emph[enmity]: every part and particle of it, if we may speak so, the least degree of it that can possibly remain in anyone, while and where there is anything of its nature, is still #emph[enmity]. It may not be as effectual and powerful in working as where it has more life and vigor, but it is still enmity. As every drop of poison is poison and will infect, and every spark of fire is fire and will burn, so it is with everything of the law of sin. The last of it, the least of it, is enmity; it will poison, it will burn. What is anything in the #emph[abstract] remains so while it has any being at all. Our #emph[apostle], who may well be supposed to have made as great progress in subduing it as anyone on earth, still, after all, cries out for deliverance as from an #emph[irreconcilable enemy] (Rom 7:24). Its lowest action, its least and most imperceptible working, is the action and working of enmity. Mortification lessens its #emph[force], but does not change its #emph[nature]. Grace changes the nature of man, but nothing can change the nature of sin. Whatever effect is worked on it, no effect is worked in it except that it is still #emph[enmity], still sin. This, then, is our state and condition because of it. “God is love” (1 John 4:8). He is so in #emph[himself], eternally excellent and desirable above all. He is so #emph[to us]\; he is so in the blood of his Son and in all its inexpressible fruits, by which we are what we are, and in which all our future hopes and expectations are wrapped up. Against #emph[this God] we carry an #emph[enmity] about with us all our days: an enmity that has this from its nature, that it is incapable of cure or reconciliation. Destroyed it may be, and it will be, but cured it cannot be. If a man has an enemy to deal with who is too mighty for him, as #emph[David] had with #emph[Saul], he may take the course David took. He may consider what it is that has provoked his enemy against him, and so set about removing the cause and making his peace (1 Sam 26:19): “If the #smallcaps[Lord] has stirred you up against me, then may He accept an offering. But if men have done it, may they be cursed in the presence of the #smallcaps[Lord]!” Whether it comes from God or from man, there is still hope of peace. But when a man has #emph[enmity] itself to deal with, nothing is to be expected except continual fighting until one party is destroyed. If it is not overcome and destroyed, it will overcome and destroy the soul.

And in this lies no small part of the #emph[power] we are inquiring into: it can admit of no terms of peace, no settlement. There may be a settlement where there is no reconciliation; there may be a #emph[truce] where there is no #emph[peace]. But with this enemy we can obtain neither the one nor the other. It is never quiet, whether conquering or conquered, which was the only kind of enemy the famous warrior of old complained of. It is vain for a man to expect any #emph[rest] from his #emph[lust] except by its death, or #emph[absolute freedom] except by his own. Some, when their corruptions are in an uproar, seek quiet by laboring to satisfy them, “making provision for the lusts of the flesh,” as the apostle speaks (Rom 13:14). This is to put out a fire with wood and oil. If all the fuel in the world, all the combustible fabric of the creation, were thrown into the fire, it would not satisfy it at all but increase it. So it is with the satisfaction given to sin by sinning: it only inflames and increases it. If a man will part with some of his goods to an enemy, that may satisfy him. But enmity will have everything, and is not one bit more satisfied than if it had received nothing at all, like the #emph[thin cows] that were no less hungry for having devoured the fat ones. You#super[+] cannot bargain with the fire to take only so much of your#super[+] houses; you#super[+] have no way except to put it out. In this case it is as it is in the contest between a wise man and a fool (Prov 29:9): “there will be raving and laughing with no rest.” Whatever frame or temper the fool is in, his persistent folly makes him troublesome. So it is with this indwelling sin. Whether it rages or laughs, whether it is in a violent uproar (as it will be on provocations and temptations, when it will be outrageous in the soul) or whether it seems pleased and content, satisfied, it is all one: there is no peace, no rest to be had with it or by it. If it had been of any other nature, some other way might have been fixed on. But since it consists in #emph[enmity], all the relief the soul has must lie in its ruin.

Secondly, it is not only said to be #emph[enmity]\; it is said to be #emph[enmity against God]. It has chosen a great enemy indeed. In various places it is set out as #emph[our enemy] (1 Pet 2:11): “abstain from fleshly lusts, which war against your soul.” They are enemies to the soul, that is, to ourselves. At times it is set out as an enemy to the Spirit that is in us: “the flesh lusts against the Spirit,” or fights against it (Gal 5:17). It fights against the Spirit, or the #emph[spiritual principle] that is in us, to conquer it; it fights against our souls to destroy them. It has special ends and designs against our souls and against the principle of grace that is in us. But its proper formal object is God: it is enmity against God. It is its work to oppose grace. To oppose our souls is a consequence of its work, which follows from what it does more than from what it intends. But its nature and formal design is to oppose God. God as the lawgiver, God as holy, God as the author of the gospel, a way of salvation by grace and not by works: this is the direct object of the law of sin. Why does it oppose duty, “so that the good we want to do, we do not do,” either as to its matter or its manner? Why does it make the soul carnal, unfit, unbelieving, unspiritual, weary, wandering? It is because of its enmity to God, with whom the soul aims to have communion in duty. It has, as it were, the command from Satan that the #emph[Assyrians] had from their king: “Do not fight with anyone, small or great, except the king of Israel” (1 Kgs 22:31). It is neither great nor small, but #emph[God himself], the King of #emph[Israel], whom sin sets itself against. There lies the secret formal reason for all its opposition to #emph[good]: because good relates to #emph[God]. Suppose a road, a routine, a way of duties is set up in which communion with God is not aimed at, but only the duty itself, as is the way of men in most of their superstitious worship. Then the opposition the law of sin raises against it will be very weak, easy and gentle. It is like the #emph[Assyrians], who attacked #emph[Jehoshaphat] because he looked like a king, but turned back from pursuing him when they found that he was not #emph[Ahab]. Because there is a show and appearance of the worship of God, sin may rise against it at first. But when the duty cries out in the heart that God is not really there, sin turns away to seek its proper enemy, God himself, elsewhere. That is why many poor creatures spend their days in grim, wearying superstitions without any great resistance from within, while others cannot be allowed to watch with Christ freely in a spiritual manner for one hour. And it is no wonder that men fight with carnal weapons for their superstitious worship #emph[outside] them, when they have no fighting against it #emph[within]. For God is not in it, and the law of sin makes no opposition to any duty, but to God in every duty. This is our state and condition. All the opposition that arises in us to anything spiritually good, whether it comes from darkness in the mind, or aversion in the will, or sloth in the affections, and all the secret arguings and reasonings in the soul that go along with them, have God himself as their direct object. The enmity lies against #emph[him]. Surely this consideration should move us to a constant, unbroken watchfulness over ourselves.

It is so also with respect to every inclination to sin, as well as to aversion from God. It is God himself who is aimed at. It is true that the pleasures, the wages of sin, greatly influence the sensual, carnal affections of men. But it is the holiness and authority of God that sin itself rises up against. It hates the yoke of the Lord. “You have grown weary of Me,” God says to sinners, and that while they are performing a great many duties. Every act of sin is a fruit of being #emph[weary of God]. So #emph[Job] tells us what lies at the bottom of the hearts of sinners: “they say to God: ‘Depart from us!’” It is enmity against him and aversion from him. Here lies the formal nature of every sin. It is an opposition to God, a throwing off of his yoke, a breaking off of the dependence the creature ought to have on the Creator. And in Rom 8:7 the apostle gives the reason why he affirms “the mind of the flesh to be enmity against God”: namely, because “it does not submit to God’s law, nor can it do so.” It never submits to God, nor will, nor #emph[can]\; its whole nature consists in opposition to him. The soul in which it is may be subject to the law of God, but this law of sin sets itself up against that law and will not be in subjection.

To show a little further the power of this law of sin from this property of its nature, that it is enmity against God, we may consider one or two things that are inseparable from it, which will further prove it.

First, it is #emph[universal]. Some contentions are limited to particular concerns: this one is about one thing, that one about another. It is not so here. The enmity is absolute and #emph[universal], as are all enmities that are grounded in the nature of the things themselves. Such enmity is against the whole kind of thing that is its object. And this enmity is such, for, first, it is #emph[universal] to all of God; and secondly, it is #emph[universal] in all of the soul.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, it is #emph[universal to all of God]. Suppose there were anything of God (his nature, his properties, his mind or will, his law or gospel, any duty of obedience to him or of communion with him) that sin had no enmity against. Then the soul might have a constant shelter and retreat within itself, by turning to that in God, to that duty toward him, to that communion with him, which sin would make no opposition to. But the enmity lies against God, and #emph[all] of God, and everything in which or by which we have to do with him. It is not subject to the law, nor to any part or particle, word or tittle of the law. Whatever is opposed to anything as such is opposed to all of it. Sin is enmity to God as God, and therefore to all of God. Not his goodness, not his holiness, not his mercy, not his grace, not his promises: there is nothing of him that it does not rise against, and no duty, private or public, in the heart or in external works, that it does not oppose. And the #emph[nearer] (if I may say so) anything is to God, the greater is sin’s enmity to it. The more spirituality and holiness there is in anything, the greater its enmity. Whatever has most of God has most of its opposition. Of those in whom this law is predominant God says, “you#super[+] neglected all my counsel, and wanted none of my correction” (Prov 1:25). It is not this or that part of God’s counsel, his mind or will, that is opposed, but “all his counsel.” Whatever he calls for or guides to, in every particular of it, is all set at nothing, and none of his correction is attended to. A man would not think it very strange that sin should maintain an enmity against God in his #emph[law], which comes to judge it and condemn it. But it raises a greater enmity against him in his gospel, in which he offers mercy and pardon as a deliverance from it. And that is only because more of the glorious properties of God’s nature, more of his excellencies and condescension, are shown in the gospel than in the law.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it is #emph[universal in all of the soul]. If this law of sin had been content to subdue any one #emph[faculty] of the soul, if it had left any one at liberty, any one affection free from its yoke and bondage, it might possibly have been opposed or subdued with more ease. But when Christ comes with his spiritual power on the soul to conquer it for himself, he has no quiet landing place. He can set foot on no ground that he does not have to fight for and conquer. Not the mind, not an affection, not the will: all is held against him. And when grace has made its entrance, sin will still dwell #emph[in all its borders]. If anything in the soul were in perfect freedom and liberty, a #emph[stand] might be made there to drive sin from all the rest of its strongholds. But it is #emph[universal], and wars in the whole soul. The mind has its own darkness and vanity to wrestle with; the will its own stubbornness, obstinacy and perverseness; every affection its own contrariness and aversion from God, and its sensuality, to deal with. So one cannot give relief to another as they ought; they have, as it were, their hands full at home. That is why our #emph[knowledge] is imperfect, our #emph[obedience] weak, our love not unmixed, our fear not pure, our delight not free and noble. But I must not dwell on these particulars, or I could show at length how this principle of enmity against God is spread through the whole soul.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, to this might be added its #emph[constancy]. It is constant to itself; it does not waver; it has no thought of yielding or giving up, in spite of the powerful opposition made to it by both the law and the gospel, as will be shown later.]

This, then, is a #emph[third evidence] of the power of sin, taken from its nature and properties. To illustrate it I have fixed on only #emph[one instance], namely, that it is #emph[enmity against God], and that universal and constant. If we were to enter on a full description of it, it would require more space and time than we have allotted to this whole subject. What has been said might give us a little sense of it, if it is the will of God, and stir us up to watchfulness. What could be a sadder thing to consider than that we should constantly carry about with us what is enmity against God, and that not in this or that particular, but in all that he is and in all in which he has revealed himself? I cannot say it is well with those who #emph[do not find it]. It is well indeed with those in whom it is weakened and its power lessened. But as for those who say it “is not in them, they only deceive themselves, and the truth is not in them.”
